    "narrative": "For the June 6th slate, **Corbin Carroll** leads the charge with an elite 85.94 score, buoyed by a favorable matchup against Littell and a prime #2 spot in the order. His ability to anchor our stolen base production makes him a near-automatic start, even with a slight drag from opposing catcher efficiency. **Josh Naylor** (77.77) and **Brice Turang** (75.06) provide the secondary tier of high-floor production; **Brice Turang** in particular benefits significantly from a favorable wind forecast and a projected top-of-the-order assignment against Agnos, maximizing his opportunities to weaponize his sprint speed. **James Wood** rounds out the elite tier at 73.03, as his elite 0.401 xwOBA suggests he is primed to outperform his matchup against Rodriguez, despite the handedness disadvantage.\n\nThe bench decision process was tightest between **Seiya Suzuki** (54.54) and **Austin Riley** (45.23). While **Austin Riley** carries significant power upside, the optimizer favored **Seiya Suzuki** for his park-adjusted efficiency and slightly better matchup against Roupp. **Austin Riley** was penalized heavily by the sub-3.50 xERA of Ashcraft and a rigorous catcher defensive metric that limits his base-running upside, cementing his status as a high-value reserve for today. We remain cautious regarding **Dalton Rushing** and **Alex Freeland**; their scores of 56.17 and 52.19, respectively, are heavily contingent on unofficial lineup placements. Should either be bumped from their projected order, their efficiency floor will drop, and we may look toward the bench for more stable output. Finally, please note that **Mickey Moniak** and **Giancarlo Stanton** remain sidelined on the IL, leaving our active depth limited as we navigate these mid-range matchups.",
